  <!-- Overview -->
  <section class="section" id="overview">
    <h2>Overview</h2>
    <p>
      Mobile banking apps are often among the most complex products people use daily — yet they're opened for the simplest of reasons: check balance, send money, see a card transaction. UnicBank is a neobank app concept for iOS that inverts this relationship. Core functionality is available in one tap from the home screen, while advanced features — savings goals, FX rates, card controls — unfold progressively as users deepen their relationship with the app.
    </p>
    <p>
      The project covered the full UX/UI process: user research and competitive analysis, end-to-end user flow definition, 65 screens of wireframes and high-fidelity designs, a reusable component library, and a clickable Figma prototype prepared for developer handoff.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- Research -->
  <section class="section" id="research">
    <h2>Research</h2>
    <p>
      To ground the design in real needs, I conducted user interviews and gathered observations from people who use mobile banking several times a week. Participants ranged from students managing a first account to professionals handling multiple currencies and international transfers.
    </p>

    <h3>Key themes</h3>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Speed above all.</strong> The most common action — checking the current balance — should require zero navigation. Users resent unlocking an app only to tap through three screens to see a number.</li>
      <li><strong>Trust through familiarity.</strong> Financial apps carry higher stakes than social apps. Native iOS patterns reduce cognitive load and signal reliability; unfamiliar gestures or layouts create hesitation around money.</li>
      <li><strong>Hidden power features.</strong> Card management, spending analytics, and international transfer settings are frequently needed but almost universally buried. Users either didn't know features existed or gave up finding them.</li>
    </ul>

    <div class="callout">
      "I just want to see my balance and send money. I don't need three onboarding screens every time I open the app."
    </div>
  </section>

