  <!-- User Flows -->
  <section class="section" id="flows">
    <h2>User flows</h2>
    <p>
      I mapped end-to-end flows for four core scenarios, charting every decision point, error state, and alternative path. Each flow was benchmarked against the competitor average number of steps.
    </p>

    <h3>1 — Onboarding & account creation</h3>
    <p>
      Identity verification is split across two moments: a short initial registration (name, email, phone) followed by ID verification triggered only when the user first needs a feature that requires it. This progressive KYC approach reduced the initial onboarding to under 90 seconds, versus the 4–7 minute competitor average.
    </p>

    <h3>2 — Home screen interaction</h3>
    <p>
      The home screen shows live balance, the last three transactions, a quick-send row of recent recipients, and a card widget — all without scrolling. Advanced widgets (savings goal progress, FX rates, spending breakdown) appear below the fold and are surfaced contextually based on usage patterns.
    </p>

    <h3>3 — Card management</h3>
    <p>
      The card widget on the home screen is tappable and expands to a card detail sheet: freeze/unfreeze, spending limits, online payments toggle, and virtual card number — all within a single bottom sheet, no separate screen stack.
    </p>

    <h3>4 — International transfers</h3>
    <p>
      Currency selection, amount entry, recipient, and confirmation are consolidated into a 3-step flow. Exchange rate and fee are shown before commitment. The flow supports both saved recipients and new ones via IBAN scan.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- Design -->
  <section class="section" id="design">
    <h2>Design</h2>
    <p>
      65 screens across all flows, built to Apple's Human Interface Guidelines. The visual language uses glass morphism — translucent card surfaces layered over a deep navy gradient — to create depth while keeping the interface legible and premium.
    </p>

    <h3>Typography</h3>
    <p>
      SF Pro throughout, following iOS weight conventions. Financial figures (balance, amounts, exchange rates) use the tabular numeric variant to prevent layout shifts during live updates. A tight weight hierarchy — Regular for body, Semibold for amounts, Bold for primary CTAs — reduces visual noise.
    </p>

    <h3>Colour</h3>
    <p>
      Deep navy (#0D1B3E) as the primary brand surface; high-contrast amber (#F5A623) for primary actions and key financial figures; a neutral light grey (#F2F4F8) for secondary surfaces and input backgrounds. The palette passes WCAG AA contrast requirements across all text sizes.
    </p>

    <h3>Home screen innovation</h3>
    <p>
      The central design decision was treating the home screen as a control panel rather than a dashboard. Every element either completes a task or starts one — nothing is purely decorative. The balance card doubles as a quick-send trigger; tapping a transaction opens its full detail without navigating away from the home context.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- UI Kit -->
  <section class="section" id="uikit">
    <h2>UI Kit</h2>
    <p>
      To support developer handoff and ensure visual consistency across 65 screens, I built a component library of 40+ reusable elements, organised into four categories.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Foundations</strong> — colour tokens, typography scale, spacing grid, icon set</li>
      <li><strong>Inputs</strong> — text fields, amount entry, search, dropdowns, toggles, segmented controls</li>
      <li><strong>Cards & surfaces</strong> — balance card, transaction row, recipient chip, notification banner</li>
      <li><strong>Navigation & overlays</strong> — tab bar variants, bottom sheets, action sheets, modals, toasts</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Each component includes all interactive states: default, focused, filled, error, disabled, and loading. Annotations document spacing values, corner radii, and animation specs for iOS native transitions.
    </p>
  </section>

  <!-- Prototype -->
  <section class="section" id="prototype">
    <h2>Prototype</h2>
    <p>
      A fully clickable Figma prototype covers all four user flows with realistic transitions — slide, sheet present, and fade — matching iOS native behaviour. The prototype was used both for internal review and for a round of informal usability walkthroughs.
    </p>
    <p>
      Handoff materials include: annotated screens with spacing and type specs, a component library linked to all frames, and a written interaction guide describing custom gestures and animation timing.
    </p>
