  <!-- Reflections -->
  <section class="section" id="reflections">
    <h2>Reflections</h2>

    <h3>1 — Simplicity is made of hundreds of small decisions</h3>
    <p>
      Every element on the home screen was challenged: does this belong here, or is it a second-tap feature? That question, repeated across every screen, is what produces a genuinely simple interface. There is no single "simple" decision — just the accumulation of many constrained ones.
    </p>

    <h3>2 — iOS guidelines accelerate rather than constrain</h3>
    <p>
      Committing fully to Apple's HIG eliminated a category of decisions entirely — transitions, sheet behaviour, typography scale — and made developer conversations faster. The constraint became a creative boundary rather than a limitation.
    </p>

    <h3>3 — Next: usability testing</h3>
    <p>
      The progressive disclosure model for KYC and advanced features is a hypothesis. The natural next step would be moderated usability sessions to validate whether users discover card controls and FX features at the right moment, or whether they need earlier signposting.
    </p>
  </section>

